 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Adams died 1848",
  "tune": "public domain: Mason died 1872",
  "arrangement": "Lowell Mason's four parts for Bethany as printed in The Sabbath Hymn and Tune Book (public domain: published 1859; Mason died 1872): his tune, alto, tenor and bass, sung one note to a syllable (the alto and tenor notes the page moves to under the tune's two slurred notes left out), nine harmony notes changed (the alto and tenor each step back after a leap where the page leaps a fourth, three times each; the bass leaps once a line; the duet loses a unison and a bare final fifth), the last note held through the closing rest, and breaths written in for beginners",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "The Sabbath Hymn and Tune Book: for the service of song in the house of the Lord, edited by Lowell Mason, Edwards A. Park and Austin Phelps (New York: Mason Brothers, 1859), p. 244, \"Bethany. 6s & 4s.\", for hymn 989, in G, printed in six-crotchet bars and written here as 3/4; verse 1 as printed below it, its repeated line sung twice as the music writes it out; Princeton Theological Seminary Library's 1859 copy at archive.org/details/sabbtune00maso (scan page n249), read at higher resolution from the Boston Public Library's copy of a later Oliver Ditson printing from the same plates (M2123.M37 S33 1859x) at archive.org/details/sabbathhymntuneb00maso_0 (scan page n247); checked note for note against Hymns and Songs of Praise for Public and Social Worship, edited by Roswell D. Hitchcock, Zachary Eddy and Philip Schaff (New York: A. S. Barnes, 1874), p. 288, \"Bethany. Arr. by Lowell Mason\", Princeton Theological Seminary Library's copy at archive.org/details/hymnssongso00hitc (scan page n295), a new engraving in 6/8 that prints the same four parts"
 },
